<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- The INTERNET permission is required for development. Specifically,
         the Flutter tool needs it to communicate with the running application
         to allow setting breakpoints, to provide hot reload, etc.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>
    <!-- Every Android row of the device matrix talks to the disposable Synapse
         in `tooling/docker` over plain HTTP — `http://10.0.2.2:8008` from an
         emulator, `http://localhost:8008` through `adb reverse` from a phone —
         and the Patrol suite additionally stands up loopback stub servers inside
         the app's own process for the bridge, transcription and embedding
         endpoints it has nothing real to point at.

         None of that reaches a socket without this. Weft targets API 36, and
         from API 28 the platform's default network security config sets
         `cleartextTrafficPermitted="false"`: every one of those connections
         fails with "CLEARTEXT communication to 10.0.2.2 not permitted by
         network security policy", which surfaces as a homeserver that appears
         never to have come up. Verified against the merged manifest, which
         carried no `usesCleartextTraffic` and no `networkSecurityConfig` before
         this: nothing in the Flutter embedding or in any plugin supplies one.

         Debug only, and that is the point rather than an oversight. Patrol
         builds and installs the debug variant, so the whole suite is covered;
         a release Weft still refuses cleartext, which for a messenger is the
         correct default and is not something a test harness should relax. -->
    <application android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"/>
</manifest>
